# Generated app definitions live at the repo root and carry real secrets in
# cleartext (admin passwords, SECRET_KEYs of live installations). They are
# output, not source — `zimapp.py convert` reproduces them at any time.
# blueprints/ is NOT affected: that is the source these are generated from.
/*.yml
!zimapp-src.yml
!zimapp-zimaos.yml
# Exception with a reason: invoiceninja.yml is not the output of a conversion —
# the converter cannot produce it (see README), it is source, and shipping it is
# the whole point. Its secrets are generated for the file and marked "change
# this" three times. ⚠️ They are the values the verification install ran with,
# so that the shipped bytes are the tested bytes — whoever keeps that install
# has to change the admin password, because this file is public.
!invoiceninja.yml
# Same reasoning for soulsync.yml, minus the warning: `convert` gets it 80 % of
# the way, but the eleven mounts that make the app useful are hand-wired against
# ZimaOS' own media folders, so it is source too. It contains NO secrets — the
# app has no credentials in its compose at all, and nothing in it was generated
# for a live installation.
!soulsync.yml
# slskd.yml ships for the same reason and comes with the invoiceninja caveat back:
# it DOES carry secrets — a web UI password and an API key, both generated for
# this file and marked "change this" in its header. They are the values the
# verification install ran with, so the shipped bytes are the tested bytes.
# ⚠️ Whoever keeps that install has to change both, because this file is public.
!slskd.yml
# jellyfin.yml: same family, no secrets at all (the admin account is created in
# the setup wizard on first start, not in the file).
!jellyfin.yml
# onlyoffice.yml: same family. The converter cannot touch it (upstream's compose
# carries a `build:` block), so it is source. Its one secret is a deliberate
# placeholder — `change-me-before-you-connect-anything`, the value the
# verification install ran with, so the shipped bytes are the tested bytes.
# ⚠️ Whoever keeps that install has to replace it, because this file is public.
!onlyoffice.yml
# kopia.yml: same family, and the one file here that carries NO secret at all —
# its three password variables ship empty on purpose. That is not an oversight to
# be fixed before publishing: with them empty the server answers 401 to every
# request, so an unconfigured install is locked rather than open. The shipped
# bytes are the tested bytes; the verification install got its values afterwards,
# through `zimapp update`, exactly the way a user sets them.
!kopia.yml

__pycache__/
*.pyc
gen.log

# Internal handover notes: infrastructure details of the machine this was built
# on, plus a section describing what an unauthenticated caller can do with an
# exposed instance. Everything a user of this repo needs is in README.md.
STATUS.md

# Local Claude Code settings.
.claude/
